{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795260087,"nonce":"I2gTnudG9BFGv7NmXIJ2sCPj+Lg=","message":"Physics, decided 2026-11-21. Study of Chimpmonk's engine (chimpmonk/chimpmonk/physics, ~13k lines of Haxe):\n- One 3D world; a body's degrees of freedom are inferred from its shapes (DoFMask Planar: X, Y linear and Z angular, when every shape has zero depth, no hull and one localZ; Full3D otherwise). Shapes are 2D outlines extruded by an optional depth, plus 3D hulls. This is the one API for 2D and 3D; keep it.\n- Struct-of-arrays throughout (bodies, shapes, contacts, broad-phase tree, joints), generation-checked handles, capacity padded to 4 for SIMD; Box2D v3 style soft-step solver with 4 substeps; dynamic AABB tree; GJK, EPA and face clipping; greedy graph colouring; conservative-advancement TOI; union-find islands and sleep; five joint kinds; bit-for-bit replay and full snapshots; allocation-checked step and a large check suite in scalar and SIMD builds.\n- HashLink-specific: hl.Bytes FloatBuffer, hl.NativeArray, ash.simd Float32x4, sys.thread pool. Its core was tuned against the Ash runtime (the JIT scalarizes inline Float32x4; on stock HashLink each Float32x4 op allocates 16 bytes).\n- Hot spots: every shape queries the whole tree each substep (no move buffer or pair cache); GJK/EPA/clipping over arrays of Vec3 objects; O(n) wakeBody; a closure per parallel job per step; pairs and contacts beyond limits dropped silently.\n\nDecisions:\n1. Physics defaults to the GPU solve where the device has Compute; a world can opt into the deterministic CPU solve (bit-for-bit replay, networking).\n2. Copy Chimpmonk's physics whole into a first-party hxnme physics plugin, then refactor in place with its check suite running throughout. Portability needs hxn typed buffers (cb750a2), a portable Float32x4 with a scalar path on hxcpp, and a worker pool that also runs in threaded wasm. Because the core was optimized against Ash, Ash performance is the baseline it must keep; other runtimes (stock HashLink, hxcpp, wasm) are tuned separately and may need different code paths.\n3. Benchmark harnesses from day 1, before porting choices are made, to judge whether the architecture holds:\n   - Scenes that scale: stacking pyramids, ragdoll crowds, many loose bodies, joint chains, planar and 3D variants.\n   - Per-stage timings (broad phase, narrow phase, colouring, solve, integration, TOI, islands), CPU and GPU paths side by side, plus allocations, memory footprint and GPU upload/readback volume.\n   - Chimpmonk on Ash as the reference, then each runtime: Ash hybrid/JIT, stock HashLink, hxcpp, wasm.\n   - Both machines, because they differ where this matters: the Mac is Apple Silicon with unified memory (CPU and GPU share memory, so buffer residency and copies cost differently), the NUC is Intel Alder Lake with Iris Xe graphics and heterogeneous P/E cores (thread-pool placement and an integrated GPU with a different driver model).\n   - Harnesses are Haxe programs run like the other hxn checks; results go into issues, never into code or comments.\n\nChimpmonk and Ashui are to move their cores onto hxn; this plugin becomes the physics they share.","files":null}]}